    "chunk_size_bytes": 1813,
    "chunk_text": "HSBC Ceylon Literary and Arts Festival Opens with Call to Inspire a New Generation\n\nThe third edition of the HSBC Ceylon Literary and Arts Festival opened on the banks of Beira Lake in Colombo (Cinnamon Lakeside) on Friday with a strong call to harness the power of arts and literature to inspire Sri Lanka\u2019s next generation.\n\nDelivering the welcome address, Festival Director and Co-founder Ajai Vir Singh said the event was conceptualised three years ago at a time when the country was grappling with multiple crises and social strain.\n\nHe added:\u201dSri Lanka has gone through a tremendously tough time over the last five years,\u201d he said. \u201cThis festival was created to provide a platform for mainstream art, literature, music and film \u2014 to give voice to our creatives and to encourage youth engagement at a time when it was most needed.\u201d\n\nSingh noted that the festival aimed to bring communities back together through cultural exchange, while also enhancing Sri Lanka\u2019s global cultural footprint.\n\nHe thanked title sponsor HSBC and partners including the Indian High Commission, the Swiss Embassy, the British Council and other cultural institutions for supporting the initiative.\n\nThe festival featured more than 45 authors, writers and personalities, alongside art exhibitions, film screenings and a jazz performance by an international ensemble from Milan. An art exhibition by the John Keells Foundation is also on display at the venue.\n\nChief Executive Officer of HSBC Sri Lanka, Mark Surgenor, addressing the gathering, said the festival had grown significantly since its inception.\n\n\u201cThis is the third edition, bigger than before, with more authors and more countries represented,\u201d he said. \u201cThe more voices we bring together, the richer and more interesting our conversations become.\u201d"
